WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.308 --> 00:00:03.131
- Hello, Believe Nation and
the start of a new series

00:00:03.131 --> 00:00:05.236
we're calling the Did You Know series.

00:00:05.236 --> 00:00:08.661
Our first video is going to
be on nine surprising facts

00:00:08.661 --> 00:00:10.539
about Tony Robbins.

00:00:10.539 --> 00:00:11.587
Hope you enjoy.

00:00:11.587 --> 00:00:14.337
(loud swooshing)

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:17.378
Fact number one, he started in

00:00:17.378 --> 00:00:18.737
the personal development field

00:00:18.737 --> 00:00:20.486
when he was working for Jim Rohn

00:00:20.486 --> 00:00:24.599
at the age of 17 selling
his motivational seminars.

00:00:24.599 --> 00:00:26.866
- I was working for Jim Rohn, the speaker,

00:00:26.866 --> 00:00:28.511
and I was 17 years old.

00:00:28.511 --> 00:00:32.157
I had long hair, minestrone
soup acne on my face

00:00:32.157 --> 00:00:35.094
and I was trying to call on
Bear Stearns type of people

00:00:35.094 --> 00:00:36.272
and convince them why they should go to

00:00:36.272 --> 00:00:38.182
this man's seminar and be more successful.

00:00:38.182 --> 00:00:40.801
I was driving a 1968 Volkswagen

00:00:40.801 --> 00:00:43.635
that I had earned at
$40 a week as a janitor.

00:00:43.635 --> 00:00:46.917
The only way I did it was
park far from the building

00:00:46.917 --> 00:00:48.513
and then go in and I love people

00:00:48.513 --> 00:00:50.422
and I believe when I put myself in state

00:00:50.422 --> 00:00:51.531
and I was able to influence people

00:00:51.531 --> 00:00:53.773
that were far more successful
than I was at the time.

00:00:53.773 --> 00:00:57.432
I had a dear, dear friend
and mentor named Jim Rohn

00:00:57.432 --> 00:01:00.552
who was my teacher when
I was 17 years old.

00:01:00.552 --> 00:01:02.320
He's somebody that I sent out some videos.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:03.993
I'll probably attach
some things to this video

00:01:03.993 --> 00:01:05.191
or another one about him just

00:01:05.191 --> 00:01:06.891
to honor him and his teachings.

00:01:06.891 --> 00:01:08.800
But he gave me a way of looking at life

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:11.573
that allowed me to not
ask life would be easier,

00:01:11.573 --> 00:01:13.860
but to ask that I be better.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:17.233
He got me to realize that
the secret to life was

00:01:17.233 --> 00:01:20.240
to work harder on myself
than my job or anything else

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:22.474
because then I'd have
something to give people.

00:01:22.474 --> 00:01:24.637
And he really shaped me
and he just passed away

00:01:24.637 --> 00:01:27.717
and I was thinking wow, I'm
going to be 49 years old.

00:01:27.717 --> 00:01:30.050
I met him when I was 17.

00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:33.333
Think of the fact that my
journey is just beginning

00:01:33.333 --> 00:01:34.895
and think of all the impact that just

00:01:34.895 --> 00:01:36.421
a couple ideas had on my life

00:01:36.421 --> 00:01:38.514
and want to really continue to to do that

00:01:38.514 --> 00:01:41.338
for you or anybody you care about, so.

00:01:41.338 --> 00:01:43.999
- Fact number two, what
made his early career

00:01:43.999 --> 00:01:45.950
was when he challenged a psychiatrist

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:48.205
saying that he could cure
someone's snake phobia

00:01:48.205 --> 00:01:49.680
in a matter of minutes.

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:50.968
- So, there's a famous
story about something

00:01:50.968 --> 00:01:52.490
I've forgotten now, but it was somebody

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:53.641
had some kind of phobia, what was it?

00:01:53.641 --> 00:01:55.062
- Snake phobia, yeah, yeah.
- Snake phobia.

00:01:55.062 --> 00:01:57.125
And the psychiatrist said
it'd take seven years

00:01:57.125 --> 00:01:58.396
and you did it in 15 minutes.
- Yeah.

00:01:58.396 --> 00:01:59.642
But what made my career was that

00:01:59.642 --> 00:02:01.168
I was attacked by the psychiatrist

00:02:01.168 --> 00:02:03.678
on the radio saying you're
a liar, your a charlatan.

00:02:03.678 --> 00:02:05.577
People like you should not
be allowed on the radio.

00:02:05.577 --> 00:02:08.095
You can't do this and so,
I was a real rebel frankly,

00:02:08.095 --> 00:02:09.272
and I was, like, in his face saying,

00:02:09.272 --> 00:02:10.768
great, are you a scientist?

00:02:10.768 --> 00:02:12.332
If you are then test your hypothesis.

00:02:12.332 --> 00:02:13.285
Give me your patient.

00:02:13.285 --> 00:02:14.730
I'll be at the Holiday Inn tomorrow night.

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:15.746
So, it was like this shootout

00:02:15.746 --> 00:02:16.985
and it was like a movie.

00:02:16.985 --> 00:02:18.152
He brought her, I wiped it out,

00:02:18.152 --> 00:02:19.504
I wrapped the snake around her.

00:02:19.504 --> 00:02:21.890
A phobia is an uncontrollable
physical response

00:02:21.890 --> 00:02:23.811
to some stimulus, you
know, it's not just fear.

00:02:23.811 --> 00:02:25.498
It's shaking, spitting.
- Right, right.

00:02:25.498 --> 00:02:26.351
- At the end she was totally fine.

00:02:26.351 --> 00:02:27.599
He worked on her for seven years.

00:02:27.599 --> 00:02:30.017
So, that became my
signature, was to challenge.

00:02:30.017 --> 00:02:31.257
And then gradually I grew 'cause

00:02:31.257 --> 00:02:32.923
I was really just trying to prove

00:02:32.923 --> 00:02:34.528
to everybody and myself as well

00:02:34.528 --> 00:02:37.271
that I was worthy rather than maturing

00:02:37.271 --> 00:02:38.693
a little bit and just knowing there

00:02:38.693 --> 00:02:41.854
are skill sets that are
valuable for everybody.

00:02:41.854 --> 00:02:44.089
- Fact number three, in the 1980s

00:02:44.089 --> 00:02:45.734
he used television infomercials

00:02:45.734 --> 00:02:48.274
